The `alarm()` handler never runs, because nothing in the class calls `setAlarm()`. The `setTimeout` it uses in its place doesn't survive the object being evicted.

**Bugs**

1. **`setTimeout` instead of `setAlarm`.** An idle Durable Object is evicted after a short time, and any timer still waiting goes with it. A one-hour `setTimeout` will almost never fire. Schedule the flush with `this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(...)`, which is saved to storage and survives eviction and restarts.
2. **A new timer on every request.** Each `fetch` adds one more flush, so 100 requests give 100 POSTs. An object has one alarm at a time, and calling `setAlarm()` replaces it. Check `getAlarm()` first and only set one if none is pending. Otherwise steady traffic keeps pushing the flush an hour into the future and it never runs.
3. **Failed sends aren't retried.** An alarm is only retried when `alarm()` throws. `fetch` doesn't throw on a 4xx or 5xx response, so a sink error counts as success. Check `res.ok` and throw if it's false.
4. **`pending` is never cleared.** Every flush sends the whole list again. If you clear it after the POST, items added while the POST was in flight get lost, so remove only the items that were sent.
5. **An empty list is still sent.** When `pending` is unset, the body is the string `"null"`. Return early if there's nothing to send.
6. **Nothing writes to `pending`.** `fetch` only updates `count`. Either `fetch` should add to `pending`, or the code that does is missing from this snippet.
7. **Alarms can run more than once.** After a retry or a crash, the sink can receive the same batch twice. Send a batch ID so the sink can drop duplicates.

**Suggested version** (it keeps the key-value storage calls you already use):

```ts
const FLUSH_DELAY_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000;

export class Room extends DurableObject {
  async fetch(request: Request) {
    const count = ((await this.ctx.storage.get<number>("count")) ?? 0) + 1;
    const pending = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    pending.push(String(count)); // replace with the real event payload
    this.ctx.storage.put({ count, pending }); // one atomic write

    if ((await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm()) === null) {
      await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_DELAY_MS);
    }
    return new Response(String(count));
  }

  async alarm() {
    await this.flush();
  }

  async flush() {
    const pending = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    if (pending.length === 0) return;

    const batchId = crypto.randomUUID();
    const res = await fetch("https://example.com/sink", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Idempotency-Key": batchId },
      body: JSON.stringify(pending),
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`sink returned ${res.status}`); // runtime retries the alarm

    // Remove only what was sent. Items added during the POST stay.
    const current = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    const rest = current.slice(pending.length);
    await this.ctx.storage.put("pending", rest);
    if (rest.length > 0) await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_DELAY_MS);
  }
}
```

Built-in alarm retries are limited. If the sink can be down for a long time, catch the error in `alarm()` and call `setAlarm()` with your own backoff. If the class uses SQLite storage, a `pending` table with one row per item works better than rewriting the whole array on every request.

To review this, I opened the Durable Objects capability group and read its skill and rules reference. I checked your code against that guidance and didn't call any of the Cloudflare API tools.
